    {"id": "CE-074", "language": "python", "snippet": "from functools import reduce\nfrom operator import mul\n\ndef product(nums):\n    return reduce(mul, nums, 1)", "purpose": "Reduce a list to its product using functools.reduce", "explanation": "reduce applies a binary function cumulatively across the iterable, starting from an initial value. mul is operator.mul = lambda a, b: a*b. The initial 1 is the multiplicative identity, ensuring product([]) = 1. Python 3.8+ has math.prod which is faster and clearer for this specific use. reduce is general; prod is special-purpose.", "key_concepts": ["reduce / fold", "operator module", "identity element"]},
    {"id": "CE-075", "language": "python", "snippet": "from itertools import groupby\n\nrows = [\n    {'team': 'A', 'name': 'Alice'},\n    {'team': 'A', 'name': 'Bob'},\n    {'team': 'B', 'name': 'Carol'},\n]\nrows.sort(key=lambda r: r['team'])\nfor team, group in groupby(rows, key=lambda r: r['team']):\n    names = [r['name'] for r in group]\n    print(team, names)", "purpose": "itertools.groupby clusters consecutive equal-key items", "explanation": "Critical caveat: groupby only groups CONSECUTIVE matches, so you must sort first by the same key. The output is (key, iterator) pairs; the iterator is single-pass (consume it before the next iteration). Same shape as SQL GROUP BY conceptually but applied to in-memory iterables.", "key_concepts": ["itertools.groupby", "must sort first", "iterator-yielding pattern"]},
    # ---- Errors + edge cases -------------------------------------------
    {"id": "CE-076", "language": "python", "snippet": "class TimeoutError(Exception):\n    pass\n\nclass NetworkError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, host: str, port: int, msg: str):\n        super().__init__(f'{host}:{port}: {msg}')\n        self.host = host\n        self.port = port\n\ntry:\n    raise NetworkError('example.com', 443, 'connection refused') from OSError('ECONNREFUSED')\nexcept NetworkError as e:\n    print(e.host, e.__cause__)", "purpose": "Custom exceptions with structured fields and exception chaining", "explanation": "Subclass Exception (or a more specific built-in) to define custom errors. Storing host/port as attributes lets callers inspect them programmatically. `raise X from Y` sets __cause__ and prints both tracebacks; preserves the lower-level cause without losing the higher-level context. Use `from None` to suppress the cause chain.", "key_concepts": ["custom exceptions", "exception chaining", "raise from"]},
